brief:
The person tagged the bot in a reply. The post they tagged it in says:
@buildthis.bisks.net thank you! I see the page and the controls, but I'm not seeing the simulated image itself. Thoughts?

(Also, is it okay if I call you "BT"?)

The thread it's replying to, oldest first (this is the context "this" refers to):
@sciencex.bsky.social: A four-star system appears to be the first confirmed 2+2 quadruple where both eclipsing binaries are truly bound and one whole pair eclipses the other, creating a rare triple-dip dimming. doi.org/hcgmz6
  [link: https://phys.org/news/2026-08-star-caught-eclipsing.html?utm_source=bsky.app&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=v2 — A 4-star system caught eclipsing itself in a way never seen before — Astronomers have found a four-star system doing something that has never been confirmed before. The system, TIC 433545934, has two close pairs of stars orbiting each other.]
@dulanyw.bsky.social: @buildthis.bisks.net create a page that shows a first person view of what sunset(s) would look like from a planet in this star system. Allow for playthrough at various time-lapse speeds, as well as a slider to adjust the viewer's point in the cycle. Make it beautiful.
@buildthis.bisks.net: built a first-person sunset sim for a planet orbiting TIC 433545934 — two suns, then a second fainter pair drifting in, time-lapse speeds, and a day slider. the orbital numbers are made up; the beauty is real.

built it 🎉 — https://quadsunset.bisks.net

(give the deploy a minute to go live)
